<<<
lay & lie. 1. Verbs. To lay is transitive only (=put to rest), & makes laid; to lie is intransitive only (=be at or come to rest), & makes lay, lain, never laid.
===
**lay & lie.** 1. Verbs. *To lay* is transitive only (=put to rest), & makes *laid*; *to lie* is intransitive only (=be at or come to rest), & makes *lay*, *lain*, never *laid*.
>>>

<<<
sometimes making its way into print, is the use of laid, which belongs to the verb to lay only, for lay the past tense, & lain the p.p., of lie (we laid out on the grass, & could have laid there all day). 2.
===
sometimes making its way into print, is the use of *laid*, which belongs to the verb *to lay* only, for *lay* the past tense, & *lain* the p.p., of *lie* (*we laid out on the grass, & could have laid there all day*). 2.
>>>

<<<
Nouns. Lie & lay are both used in the senses
===
Nouns. *Lie* & *lay* are both used in the senses
>>>

<<<
that is for lie (the proper lye of the land, 1692); dlie seems also the more reasonable form, lay having issued perhaps from sailors’ & rustic talk, in which the verbs are not kept distinct.
===
that is for *lie* (*the proper lye of the land, 1692*); *lie* seems also the more reasonable form, *lay* having issued perhaps from sailors' & rustic talk, in which the verbs are not kept distinct.
>>>

<<<
layer makes layered; see -R-, -RR-.
